**WAKING FROM INNOCENT DREAMS** is {{{genre}}} with elements of magical realism. A cross between HADES, ARGENTINA and THE VOLCANO DAUGHTERS, it will also appeal to readers who liked A BEAST SLINKS TOWARDS BEIJING. This multigenerational family saga, complete at 110,000 words, spans nearly four decades and follows a boy who survived and a ghost who remembers. 

Nelson is loved but looks nothing like the rest of his family. He longs to meet the one person he believes will make him whole, while trying to ignore the ghost that won’t leave him alone. Then a positive DNA test reveals him to be Roberto, the son of Salvadoran revolutionaries who, as an infant, was *desaparecido* by a Honduran death squad. At a miraculous reunion, Nelson is greeted by his grandmother, father, siblings, and their larger-than-life lore. Everyone is ready to welcome him home except the one person who was disappeared with him: his mother.

After he travels to Central America, his past and present begin to blend, thunderstorms drown out discontent, and living rooms expand to fit the entire neighborhood. Over the years, as he discovers a life taken from him, he struggles to balance the demands of his two lives, leaving him feeling *ni de aquí ni de allá*. Just as Nelson comes to terms with the cost of his disappearance, a betrayal forces him to make an impossible choice: protect the family he has dreamed of since childhood, or accept the truth his ghost and siblings have been pushing him toward.

Since the 1950s, governments throughout Latin America have abducted more than 100,000 people as a strategy of intimidation and control. Only a small handful, almost all children, reunite with their families. Of those, a tiny fraction get a second chance to grow up with the family they were taken from. As one of El Salvador’s Disappeared Children, I am part of that tiny fraction, and my manuscript may be the first English-language novel about forced disappearances written by someone who survived.

My writing has appeared in _NACLA Report on the Americas_, and I attended Under the Volcano, where I took Jennifer Clement's Writing of Witness master class. Recently, an acquiring editor at Primero Sueño praised a sample of my manuscript for being fresh and new. My story has been featured in the BBC World Service, _Los Angeles Times_, _Christian Science Monitor_, and _Huffington Post_, and I am completing a documentary film, IDENTIFYING NELSON/BUSCANDO A ROBERTO, which offers natural co-marketing opportunities.

